<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- The window's namespace, in a second assembly. Both halves of the tray
         say AdlAgent.Tray because they are one program to whoever is reading
         them; what is split is the target framework, not the idea. -->
    <RootNamespace>AdlAgent.Tray</RootNamespace>
    <Description>What the tray's window shows and what its buttons do, with no window in it: the next-step line, the station rows, and the settings a technician types.</Description>
    <!-- net10.0, from Directory.Build.props, and that is the whole point of
         this project existing. These types hold the decisions the window
         draws (which state the machine is in, what to tell somebody to do
         about it, which fields differ from what ADL sent, when a poll may
         replace a row somebody is typing into), and while they sat in the
         net10.0-windows tray assembly the net10.0 test project could not
         reference them, so none of it was covered by anything but reading.
         Nothing here touches WPF: the only pieces of the desktop stack it
         uses are ICommand and ObservableCollection, both of which are in the
         base framework on every platform. -->
    <!-- Not a claim about where these types run: they run anywhere, and the
         Linux head will use them. It is the runtime identifier of the head
         they reference, and this project has to be publishable for the same
         one, because the tray publishes self-contained for win-x64 and
         reaches the head through here as well as directly. Without it the
         two paths resolve the head differently and the publish stops with
         two copies of adl-agent.runtimeconfig.json. -->
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The Windows head, for the typed control link. That is the tray's only
         route to any fact, and it compiles on any operating system: the
         named pipe underneath it is the head's business, not this
         project's. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AdlAgent.Windows\AdlAgent.Windows.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
